Here are some sample SDL2 key names.  They are all case-insensitive.
Is there a list on the 'net?  Who knows?

Single-character ASCII characters have the name on their keycap.

 Return Escape Backspace Tab Space CapsLock NumLock
 PrintScreen ScrollLock Pause Insert Home PageUp Delete End PageDown
 Right Left Down Up
 F1 .. F24
 Application Power Execute Help Menu Select Stop Again Undo Cut
 Copy Paste Find Mute VolumeUp VolumeDown AltErase SysReq Cancel
 Clear Prior Return Separator Out Oper CrSel ExSel ThousandsSeparator
 DecimalSeparator CurrencyUnint CurrencySubunit BrightnessDown
 BrightnessUp DisplaySwitch KBDIllumToggle KBDIllumDown KBDIllumUp
 Eject Sleep App1 App2 AudioRewind AudioFastForward SoftLeft SoftRight
 Call EndCall

Numeric Keypad keys are two space-separated words, the first of which
is Keypad (e.g. "Keypad 1").  Except for one, which is 3 words:
"Keypad = (AS400)"
 0 .. 9 . / * - + Enter = , 00 000 ( ) { } Tab Backspace A B C D E F
 XOR ^ % < > & && | || : # Space @ ! MemStore MemRecall MemClear
 MemAdd MemSubtract MemMultiply MemDivide +/- Clear ClearEntry
 Binary Octal Decimal Hexadecimal ModeSwitch AUdioNext AudioPrev
 AudioStop AudioPlay AudioMute AudioSelect WWW Mail Calculator
 Computer

And here are a few more with multiple words:
  "Clear / Again" "Left Ctrl" "Left Shift" "Left Alt" "Left GUI"
  "Right Ctrl" "Right Shift" "Right Alt" "Right GUI"
  "Ac Search" "AC Home" "AC Back" "AC Forward" "AC Stop" "AC Refresh"
  "AC Bookmarks"

SDL may change these at any time, without notice, as far as I can
tell.  The following changes are made based on OS:

  "Left Alt" -> "Left Option" (Mac)
  "Right Alt" -> "Right Option" (Mac)
  "Left GUI" -> "Left Command" (Mac), "Left Windows" (Windows)
  "Right GUI" -> "Right Command" (Mac), "Right Windows" (Windows)
  "Application" -> "Menu" (Mac, Windows)

This means that your config file will only work on one OS, and maybe
only one version of SDL.  SDL warns of this, but provides no stable
alternatives.  Also, SDL uses linear searches, and I do not have the
patience to extract the names into a hash table or sorted array for
faster searching. Luckily, modern computer speeds have made that
inefficiency irrelevant, and parsing only happens (every) program
startup and config change.
